welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Andre Espeland a Japan Foundation  Center for Global Partnership  postdoctoral associate in East Asian  Studies at the Macmillan Center  professor aspirin specializes in  Japanese foreign policy and diplomacy in  East and Southeast Asia he is  particularly interested in how Japan's  normative ambitions of spreading human  rights and democracy fits within its  international relations with non  democratic and strategic partners in the  Association of Southeast Asian Nations  today we'll talk with professor Aspen  about his forthcoming paper entitled  normative power Japan settling for  Chinese democracy welcome professor  Osmond thank you  let's start with an overview of your  paper tell us about it so in this paper  I draw on the developments in Japanese  foreign policy right now with the new  Shinzo Abe big government sort of  staking out a new direction for Japanese  foreign policies we're more proactive  contribution to peace and security  specifically in the Asia Pacific region  and how Japan now seeks to utilize its  foreign aid program foreign aid that has  been a huge part of Japanese foreign  policy in a more strategic way to sort  of contribute more to Japanese national  interests and in this paper I'm  interested in understanding how such  foreign aid has been facilitating any  normative power of Japan the way Japan  could disseminate norms to other  countries in the region and I look  specifically on first the rule of law in  South in the South China Sea and how  Japan is sort of setting that  the normal legitimate behavior in that  region and I want to understand if this  normative power is them applicable to  more non-security rate related realms of  Japanese foreign policy such as human  rights and democracy okay and what led  you to write the paper what was the  catalyst so I was writing another  chapter on on the EU and Japan in the  sort of era of the rise of China and  potential modes of cooperation and I was  intrigued by the European Union sort of  not being able to extend its normative  power to other areas outside of it's  sort of geographical confines of the  European continent in its foreign aid  foreign trade relationships with for  instance Vietnam and Myanmar and so that  intrigued me to look into Japan which is  a major player in this region in terms  of foreign aid to see if Japan is able  to have a political impact on these  countries through these venues of  providing foreign aid okay and tell us  about your methodology what did you look  at  so the analytical model that I wanted to  employ for this is sort of a simplified  model where I am so in terms of sort of  measuring normative power normative  power being the ability of an actor to  set what what goes for normal in  international affairs its ability to  present its own worldview as compatible  with that of the communal am within  target state and just give us some  examples of what normative values would  be so the values that I'm looking at now  would be so rule of law the rule of  let's see in the South China Sea and  human rights democratization those those  values and norms and so the methodology  was I looked at joint policy speeches  where Japan meets a target state ahead  of  and in these venues every year they  convey hopes and wishes for the future  problems that they are incurred  encountering  and how they want to solve those and I'm  so I'm trying to trace if we can see  Japan framing these values and issues as  something that they see as important for  the target state to also comply with and  I sort of trace that through the years  in these dialogues to see if we can have  a or we if you find a reciprocation or a  sort of joint communique where both  parties agree to this and say that this  is something that we also strive for and  then I take that analytical model and I  try to place that on human rights and  democracy okay  Japan has a history of isolationism and  really showing little interest on the  world stage and in terms of playing an  active role and global affairs do you  think that is changing based on what  you're seeing I think this is something  that has been ongoing for a long time in  small incremental steps it's an  innovative inevitable change that Japan  has has had to take on by socializing  more into international community I  think it's been highlighted since the  now from 2012 and on the other  government has highlighted that even  more pushing this narrative that Japan  is in fact not a normal country is an  abnormal country it's a pacifist but a  passive pacifist country and it needs to  become an actively pacifist country and  actively pacifist country that can for  instance sell arms to other countries in  order to secure democracy that's sort of  the narrative that is being pushed more  and more by the growing political right  in Japan as opposed to the political  order sort of diminishing political left  that is actually pushing for this  as something uniquely positive for Japan  not something that is abnormal in that  sense and all of this I think needs to  be seen in against the backdrop of the  rise of China the so-called rise of  China and particularly the perception in  Japan and in in in the US for instance  and elsewhere of a China that is  increasingly assertive increasingly  aggressive and it sort of bent on  expansionism even hegemony okay and that  is something that Japan feels it needs  to address definitely and specifically  the political right sees that the only  way for Japan to be able to to to answer  to this so-called or this perceived  threat of the rise of China is to amend  the Constitution that the so called  peace Constitution article 9 where where  Japan renounces waging war as means to  settle an international dispute but the  cause of public opinion in Japan being  against a provision of the Constitution  you can see these other small steps that  Albert has been been taking for instance  for instance setting up the National  Security Council in 2013 the new  national security strategy the new  Development Corporation Charter in which  this proactive contribution to peace and  security is stipulated over and over  again and where this proactive  contribution to peace and security is  being linked to a more strategic  utilization of for and aid okay and  let's talk about that utilization of  foreign aid how is it changing and why  so in my opinion I don't think that the  actual practice of providing foreign aid  is changing that much I think that the  policies are changing and becoming more  quote/unquote honest they're becoming  more aligned with the actual practice  that has been ongoing for decades in  Japan Japanese foreign aid has always  been very much a  a national interest serving national  interest for which Japan has been  heavily criticized by the OECD the  Western traditional donors saying that  Japan should not provide concessional  loans it should bother cookies would  rather provide grant aid to countries it  should not build economic infrastructure  hard infrastructure it should build  social infrastructure but Japan has sort  of taken its own way and focused on  win-win situations where both the the  provider foreign aid and the recipient  of foreign aid wins and is that working  that has been working very well for  Japan I guess it's always a matter of  development actually leading to economic  development development aid leading to  economic development I think the jury's  still out on that but it's been very  successful for Japan in forging these  very close relationships with Southeast  Asian countries which after World War  Two obviously was not they're very  friendly position towards Japan Japan  being sort of the aggressor in this area  occupying many of the Southeast Asian  countries so it seems to be so much  strategic that Japan is trying to foster  friendships with these other countries  given the aggressive nature of China  would that be true that is definitely  the perception in Japan that the China  is is aggressive but China we also have  to remember the China is the main  competitor to Japan in building  infrastructure in Southeast Asia and  also in Africa China now has the the as  in sort of an answer to the Asia  Development Bank China two years ago  inaugurated the Asia infrastructure  investment bank which is led by China  which is all also sort of competitor to  the more Japanese led Asia Development  Bank focusing on building infrastructure  in this region so China it's both sort  of a threat strategically but it's also  a competitor economically and for that  in that sense concessional loans being  the main mode of providing foreign aid  for Japan is the  much an economic competitor in that  sense okay so what are some of your  findings in your paper so in my paper I  make the case that Japan can indeed be  called a sort of normative power  in the sense of setting the rule of law  at sea as the normal legitimate behavior  in the South China Sea and for that  Japan has successfully been using it for  an aid in terms of providing vessels  patrol vessels for these littoral States  Vietnam Philippines for instance but  it's also been developing the capacity  of coastal guards in Myanmar or in in  Malaysia and in Vietnam and in the  Philippines and so by helping these  states helping Japan to make sure that  these seas are ruled by by laws and not  might Japan in that sense have been  become a normative power how is China  though factoring into that sea space as  well so China in its own sense is a  normative power China is probably more  described for what it's not than what it  is it's not the sort of hegemony of the  European Union it's not the neoliberal  cosmopolitanism of the European Union  it's more of a do it I do we do together  look at me I develop this way you can do  the same as long as it doesn't clash  with my development and I think I think  that's what we're seeing now in in the  South China Sea we see a lot of  countries that have followed the route  of China developing in China's way and  now that they're clashing and this is  also sort of what's then feeding the  Japanese normative power how Japan can  can use China as the significant other  China is different  Japan is democratic Japan is rules  abiding Japan is by that definition  legitimate in that sense and look at  China China is the opposite it's  undemocratic it's a human rights  violator it's not abiding by the rule  law hence China is illegitimate  illegitimate so in that sense that that  boosts Japan's normative power with the  countries that are in disputes with  China okay and what do you conclude so I  think all I conclude on a country level  that Japan's foreign aid in terms of  facilitating democratization and human  rights adherence in countries that are  not specifically democratic and not  specifically interested in human rights  sections which ones such as a number of  ASEAN countries for instance allows for  instance Vietnam for instance Myanmar  Japanese aid geared specifically towards  human rights and democracy in that sense  will only be as effective or it will  only be as used to the extent that it is  actually leverage against Chinese  competition as soon as those two things  clash that will not be effective but on  a more regional level in ASEAN level  Japan by ascribing already ASEAN asked  sharing these values with with Japan as  as Prime Minister Abbott has been doing  saying ASEAN is democratic it's human  rights sharing and so on in effect what  that is doing it it's legitimizing a  sort of Chinese democracy it legitimized  or it it allows China to set the  threshold for what these countries need  to fulfill in order to be recognized as  democracies by Japan so effectively  saying that some of these countries not  being that democratic you can be part of  this in-group this democratic universal  values believing group by only  committing to a level of democracy on  par with China moving forward do you  think Japan should be doing perhaps  something different in terms of its  foreign aid I think what we're seeing  right now in the aid community is the  OECD duck the Western traditional donors  are  realizing that they are becoming  obsolete their way the traditional way  of providing aid is not competitive  anymore the emergence of China as a huge  a donor taking up on the old way that  Japan used to provide aid is now getting  Japan to also looking back at how it  used to provide a more win-win more  self-interest and that in turn is  getting the Western world to see ok this  is what we need to do if we want to stay  competitive in the foreign aid business  because that's what it is it's a  business we need to start think about  this more in terms of win-win and hence  the term for an aid is sort of  diminishing and it's increasingly  becoming development cooperation ok and  what are your next steps in turn in  terms of your research I know you're  here as a visiting scholar what have you  been working on while you're here and  really where are you planning on going  next so my first project was on this  European Union and Japan corporation in  the era of the rise of China what I want  to do in the future now is I'm going to  investigate more these sort of venues  that I think are being facilitated by  Japanese foreign aid in these countries  that are less than democratic and see if  these venues if the actual fact that  Japan has not been conditioning its  foreign aid with political requirements  if that can open up a venue of trust and  recognition that in the future might be  fruitful for a normative power or a way  to to sort of leverage against China  mm-hmm very interesting thank you for  being here with us today and sharing  some of your work thank you so much for  more information about professor a  splint and his research please visit our  website at macmillan report yale.edu be  sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale  [Music]

